    "excerpt": "CAPILLARIES —Capillaries are tiny (minute) vessels that connect the smallest arteries (called arterioles) to the beginnings of the smallest veins (called venules).  Oxygen and other elements are now exchanged for carbon dioxide, other gases and metabolic wastes.  These are carried through the venous system back to your...",
    "fullText": "You have two chambers on the right and two chambers on the left. — CAPILLARIES —Capillaries are tiny (minute) vessels that connect the smallest arteries (called arterioles) to the beginnings of the smallest veins (called venules).  Oxygen and other elements are now exchanged for carbon dioxide, other gases and metabolic wastes.  These are carried through the venous system back to your lungs, kidneys and colon for elimination."
