    "excerpt": "Lymph Nodes You have thousands of small septic tanks called lymph nodes throughout your body.  Your lymph nodes are bean-shaped holding tanks, or “septic tanks,” that are used by your lymph system to filter, neutralize, bond and destroy pathogens (toxins), antigens, etc.  They consist of a fibrin net, which serves as a...",
    "fullText": "supplied by the blood system to a cell. — Lymph Nodes You have thousands of small septic tanks called lymph nodes throughout your body.  Your lymph nodes are bean-shaped holding tanks, or “septic tanks,” that are used by your lymph system to filter, neutralize, bond and destroy pathogens (toxins), antigens, etc.  They consist of a fibrin net, which serves as a filter for lymph cells.  Lymph nodes range in size—from that of a small penny to almost the size of a quarter."
