    "fullText": "A large portion of a cell’s membrane wall is cholesterol. — ” The answer is that if the adrenal glands are weak or underactive in the tissue that produces these steroids, the body cannot adequately defend itself against this strong inflammation.  Lacking adequate steroids, the body then has no choice but to turn to water and electrolytes in an attempt to manage this “fire. ” But water and electrolytes also cause edema (swelling) in the area of the inflammation.  The liver will also begin to increase its production of cholesterol, thus giving the body additional anti-inflammatory compounds.  Cholesterol is one of the main ingredients of steroids.  All of these anti-inflammatory compounds are essential to the protection of a cell against the highly damaging effects of acids."
