    "excerpt": "Literally billions of these fossils have been found, but never have there been discovered any transition types, or smooth transitions, between any of these 14 types.  The first treatise that described that the basic forms of plants and animals did not evolve step-by-step, but were suddenly there, was given by Hugo de V...",
    "fullText": "Quantities can change instantaneously. — Literally billions of these fossils have been found, but never have there been discovered any transition types, or smooth transitions, between any of these 14 types.  The first treatise that described that the basic forms of plants and animals did not evolve step-by-step, but were suddenly there, was given by Hugo de Vries in his work Mutation Theory in 1886.  He described that we can clearly see (through scientific/utility analysis) how the \"impressed living form\" works itself out in the individual samples, but we cannot see how the \"die was cut\" for each whole genus.  We cannot see how the genus appeared.  Origins remain hidden from us.  We cannot ascertain how the Homo genus (Homo habilis, Homo erectus, Homo ergaster, Homo neanderthalis, archaic Homo sapien, Cro-Magnon, Homo sapien, Homo sapien sapien) appeared, yet it was suddenly there in the fossil record."
