    "excerpt": "Brian Clement, director of the Hippocrates Health Institute, is a blood type 0 and has been a vegetarian for over 40 years.  Dr.  Gabriel Cousens is a blood type 0 and has been a raw-food vegetarian for over 20 years.  There are hundreds of other examples I could site.  In my own experience in counseling people on diet...",
    "fullText": "To a degree humans have adapted. — Brian Clement, director of the Hippocrates Health Institute, is a blood type 0 and has been a vegetarian for over 40 years.  Dr.  Gabriel Cousens is a blood type 0 and has been a raw-food vegetarian for over 20 years.  There are hundreds of other examples I could site.  In my own experience in counseling people on diet, I have found the suggestions of what foods are agreeable and disagreeable based on one's blood type to be often applicable to cooked foods and rarely applicable to raw foods and raw- food eaters.  I have also found that people of blood type A tend to burn sugars ORIGINS | 135 (carbohydrates) more slowly and can generally handle more sugars in their diet.  People of blood type 0 tend to burn sugar more rapidly and do better eating less carbohydrates and more fats and/or protein for energy.  But these are not rules.  Obviously different blood groups, races, and ethnic groups share different affinities for natural foods (e. g."
