    "fullText": "To a degree humans have adapted. — northern Europeans tend to like green apples, southern Europeans tend to like red apples).  Each group has different enzymatic capabilities to digest their traditional cooked staples.  So there is a partial truth in the blood-type theory; however, the blood-type theory makes no distinction between raw or cooked foods, nor does it address blood type O's who thrive on a vegetarian, vegan or raw-food diet - indicating a flaw in its reasoning.  If we look closely, we see that eating animal foods (meat, milk, eggs), or ingesting alcohol, requires enzyme mutations present in some groups, and absent in others.  This means that these foods were added later in our history on the planet and were not originally present.  All the enzymes originally present are still with all of us now and are most amenable to digesting simple plant matter."
