    "fullText": "PREFACE 25 the demands made upon it for instant cure. — If potatoes, grain meal, rice or meat are boiled long enough, we get jelly-like slime (mucus) or paste similar to the kind that is used by bookbinders and carpenters.  This slimy substance soon turns sour, ferments, and forms a bed for fungi, molds and bacilli.  During digestion, which is nothing else but a boiling, a combustion, this slime or paste is created in the same manner, for the blood can use only the converted dextrose sugar transformed from starch.  The waste matter, this superfluous product, i. e. , this paste or slime is at first complete- 'j creted."
