    "fullText": "8. Amargo ( Quassia amara ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Amargo - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Amargo Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/amargo.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - This family of plants is characterized by the presence of quassinoids  - secondary metabolite chemicals that are responsible for a wide spectrum of - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - In the Amazon rainforest - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : There are quite a few different areas of research that scientists have been studying and documenting on amargo. In some of the earlier research amargo was reported to have antiviral activity when scientists at Texas Christian University demonstrated in 1996 that a water extract was active in vitro against cells infected with HIV. Five additional studies were published between 1998 and 2002 with va... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In the Amazon rainforest, amargo is used much in the same manner as quinine bark: for malaria and fevers and as a bitter digestive aid. It grows at lower elevations (where quinine does not) and contains many of the same antimalarial phytochemicals (... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : kills parasites reduces inflammation Wood, Bark kills lice & insects kills cancer cells Infusion: 1 cup 2-3 times daily expels worms kills leukemia cells Capsules: 1-2 g 2-3 times daily lowers cholesterol prevents tumors Maceration: 1 cup 2-3 times daily treats malaria dries secretions prevents ulcers cleanses blood kills viruses mildly laxative stimulates digestion sedates increases bile increases saliva balances blood sugar kills bacteria  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Amargo should not be used during pregnancy. Amargo has been documented in several studies to have antifertility effects in both female and male rats. Although not scientifically confirmed in humans, those actively seeking pregnancy should avoid using amargo. Large amounts of amargo can irritate the mucous membrane of the stomach and can lead to nausea and vomiting. Do not exceed recommended dosages. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
