    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique : Crassulaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Air plant, balangban, bruja, clapper bush, coirama, coirama-branca, coirama-brava, curtain plant, dipartenga, farine chaude, fel pavo, floppers, folha-da-costa, green love, hoja de aire, life leaf, live forever, mexican loveplant, miracle leaf, motta patti,...",
    "fullText": "42. Coirama ( Kalanchoe brasiliensis ) — - Famille Botanique : Crassulaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Air plant, balangban, bruja, clapper bush, coirama, coirama-branca, coirama-brava, curtain plant, dipartenga, farine chaude, fel pavo, floppers, folha-da-costa, green love, hoja de aire, life leaf, live forever, mexican loveplant, miracle leaf, motta patti, paichecara, pashipadeh, paochecara, pirarucu, potagoja, sayao, sai&atilde, o, siempre viva - Parties Utilisées : Leaves, leaf juice - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Coirama Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/coirama.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Kalanchoe is rich in alkaloids  - triterpenes  - glycosides  - flavonoids  - steroids and lipids  - They are very similar in structure and activity as two other cardiac glycosides  - antitumorous  - cancer preventative - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - The plant should not be used in pregnancy  - Though not supported by clinical research  - or by those with a lowered immune system - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. The plant should not be used in pregnancy. Though not supported by clinical research, it has traditionally been used during childbirth and may stimulate the uterus. 2. Kalanchoe has documented immune modulating actions and should not be used chronically for long periods of time, or by those with a lowered immune system. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Many of kalanchoe's traditional uses can be explained by the clinical research conducted thus far on the plant. The traditional use for infectious conditions (both internally and externally) is supported by research indicating kalanchoe leaves have antibacterial, antiviral and antifungal activity. The leaf and leaf juice have demonstrated significant in vitro antibacterial activity towards Staphyl... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Kalanchoe is somewhat of a panacea to the indigenous peoples of the Amazon; they employ it for many different purposes. The Creoles use the lightly roasted leaves for cancer and inflammations, and a leaf infusion is a popular remedy for fevers. The Palikur mix the leaf juice with coconut oil or andiroba oil and then rub it on the forehead for migra... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : kills bacteria prevents ulcers Leaves kills viruses increases urination Infusion: 1 cup twice daily kills fungi lowers cholesterol Juice: applied topically reduces fever constricts blood vessels 2-3 times daily heals wounds mildly sedative suppresses coughs blocks histamine relieves pain relaxes muscles reduces inflammation  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. The plant should not be used in pregnancy. Though not supported by clinical research, it has traditionally been used during childbirth and may stimulate the uterus. 2. Kalanchoe has documented immune modulating actions and should not be used chronically for long periods of time, or by those with a lowered immune system. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
