    "fullText": "57. Fedegoso ( Cassia occidentalis ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Fedegoso - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Fedegoso Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/fedegosa.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - acutifolia )  - While fedegoso does contain a small amount of these anthraquinones  - The main plant chemicals in fedegoso include: achrosine  - aloe-emodin  - anthraquinones  - anthrones  - apigenin  - aurantiobtusin - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - As such  - the use of this plant is contraindicated during pregnancy  - Fedegoso has demonstrated hypotensive activity in dogs and  - as such  - as medications may need to be adjusted)  - chemicals  - actions  - and toxicity of these seeds - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Fedegoso leaf extracts have demonstrated weak uterine stimulant activity and smooth-muscle relaxant actions in rats. As such, the use of this plant is contraindicated during pregnancy. 2. Fedegoso has demonstrated hypotensive activity in dogs and, as such, is probably contraindicated in people with low blood pressure. Individuals taking medications to lower their blood pressure should check with their doctor first before taking fedegoso (and monitor their blood pressure accordingly, as medications may need to be adjusted). 3. Long-term ingestion of small amounts and single high dosages of fedegoso seeds cause toxic reactions including myodegeneration and death. Do not use fedegoso seeds without the supervision of a qualified professional who is familiar with the mechanisms, chemicals, actions, and toxicity of these seeds. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Fedegoso has been the subject of recent clinical research for its beneficial effects on the liver and immune system. In the late 1970s, two research groups published three studies citing the beneficial effects of fedegoso in human patients with liver toxicity, hepatitis, and even acute liver failure. Other researchers followed up on those actions, publishing four different in vivo studies (mice an... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Fedegoso has been used as natural medicine in the rainforest and other tropical areas for centuries. Its roots, leaves, flowers, and seeds have been employed in herbal medicine around the world. In Peru, the roots are considered a diuretic, and a decoction is made for fevers. The seeds are brewed into a coffee-like beverage for asthma, and a flower... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : protects liver relieves pain Leaves detoxifies liver reduces inflammation Infusion: 1 cup twice daily kills bacteria kills cancer cells Tincture: 3-4 ml twice daily kills fungi reduces spasms Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily kills parasites reduces fever kills viruses reduces blood pressure expels worms kills insects enhances immunity cleanses blood kills germs detoxifies promotes perspiration mildly laxative  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Fedegoso leaf extracts have demonstrated weak uterine stimulant activity and smooth-muscle relaxant actions in rats. As such, the use of this plant is contraindicated during pregnancy. 2. Fedegoso has demonstrated hypotensive activity in dogs and, as such, is probably contraindicated in people with low blood pressure. Individuals taking medications to lower their blood pressure should check with their doctor first before taking fedegoso (and monitor their blood pressure accordingly, as medications may need to be adjusted). 3. Long-term ingestion of small amounts and single high dosages of fedegoso seeds cause toxic reactions including myodegeneration and death. Do not use fedegoso seeds without the supervision of a qualified professional who is familiar with the mechanisms, chemicals, actions, and toxicity of these seeds. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
