    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique : Plante Amazonienne - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Guarana, guarana kletterstrauch, guaranastruik, quarana, quarane, cupana, Brazilian cocoa, uabano, uaranzeiro - Parties Utilisées : Seed, fruit - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire R...",
    "fullText": "65. Guarana ( Paullinia cupana ) — - Famille Botanique : Plante Amazonienne - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Guarana, guarana kletterstrauch, guaranastruik, quarana, quarane, cupana, Brazilian cocoa, uabano, uaranzeiro - Parties Utilisées : Seed, fruit - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Guarana Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/guarana.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - He isolated a bitter  - white crystalline substance with a remarkable physiological action  - Von Martius named this substance guaranine  - and it was later renamed caffeine  - Many today still believe guaranine to be a unique phytochemical in guaraná  - It is  - however (according to chemists)  - caffeine - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - Not to be used during pregnancy or while breast-feeding  - cardiac disorders  - diabetes  - ulcers  - epilepsy  - and other disorders - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Not to be used during pregnancy or while breast-feeding. 2. Guaraná contains caffeine and should not be used by those who are sensitive or allergic to caffeine or xanthines. Excessive consumption of caffeine is contraindicated for persons with high blood pressure, cardiac disorders, diabetes, ulcers, epilepsy, and other disorders. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Toxicity studies with animals (in 1998) have shown that guaraná is non-toxic, even at high dosages (up to 2 g/kg of body weight). Toxicity has been reported in only one human: a female who had an existing heart condition (mitrial valve prolapse). While the Indians have been using guaraná for centuries, Western science has been slowly, but surely, validating that the indigenous uses are well-ground... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : The uses of this plant by the Amerindians predates the discovery of Brazil. South American Indian tribes (especially the Guaranis, from whence the plant's name is derived) dry and roast the seeds and mix them into a paste with water. They then use it much the same way as chocolate - to prepare various foods, drinks, and medicines. The rainforest tr... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : stimulates relieves pain Seeds increases energy enhances memory Decoction: 1 cup 1-3 times daily dilates blood vessls mildly laxative Tincture: 1-3 ml 2-3 times daily increases urination increases libido Capsules: 1-2 g 2-3 times daily soothes nerves kills bacteria Standardized Extracts: fights free radicals thins blood follow label directions reduces weight  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Not to be used during pregnancy or while breast-feeding. 2. Guaraná contains caffeine and should not be used by those who are sensitive or allergic to caffeine or xanthines. Excessive consumption of caffeine is contraindicated for persons with high blood pressure, cardiac disorders, diabetes, ulcers, epilepsy, and other disorders. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
