    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique : Plante Amazonienne - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Jaborandi, indian hemp, jaborandi-do-norte, catai-guacu, ibiratai, pimenta-de-cachorro, arruda do mato, arruda brava, jamguarandi, juarandi - Parties Utilisées : Leaves, roots - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal ...",
    "fullText": "73. Jaborandi ( Pilocarpus jaborandi ) — - Famille Botanique : Plante Amazonienne - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Jaborandi, indian hemp, jaborandi-do-norte, catai-guacu, ibiratai, pimenta-de-cachorro, arruda do mato, arruda brava, jamguarandi, juarandi - Parties Utilisées : Leaves, roots - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Jaborandi Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/jaborand.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - to folklore use  - and then into modern medicine based upon natural chemicals found in the plant  - In 1875  - In 1876  - The mixture of pilocarpine and another natural product  - physostigmine  - remains to this day one of the mainstay drugs in ophthalmology  - Interestingly - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - An overdose may cause such symptoms as flushing  - profuse sweating and salivation  - urinary frequency  - nausea  - rapid pulse  - contracted pupils  - diarrhea or fatal pulmonary edema  - The plant may induce bradycardia - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Pilocarpine has shown to increase the rate of birth defects in animal studies. Jaborandi should not be taken during pregnancy or while breastfeeding. 2. Both jaborandi and pilocarpine may cause headaches and can irritate the stomach and cause vomiting and nausea. An overdose may cause such symptoms as flushing, profuse sweating and salivation, urinary frequency, nausea, rapid pulse, contracted pupils, diarrhea or fatal pulmonary edema. 3. The plant may induce bradycardia. Those with cardiac or circulatory conditions should not take jaborandi. 4. Jaborandi may induce dehydration due to excessive perspiration and urination. If using jaborandi, electrolyte and fluid status must be monitored and maintained. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : There are well over a thousand clinical studies published on pilocarpine. As with most plant-based drugs, however, the use of the whole natural plant fell out of use as a natural remedy (and failed to attract further research efforts) in favor of the single isolated active ingredient that was turned into a prescription drug. The PDR for Herbal Medicines indicates that the effects of jaborandi leav... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In 1570, Gabriel Soares de Souza, a European observer, first recorded that the Guarani Indians of Brazil were using jaborandi to treat mouth ulcers. In the 1630's two Dutch West Indian Company scientists documented other Brazilian Indians using it as a tonic or panacea for colds and flu, a remedy against gonorrhea and kidney stones and found that i... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : reduces glacoma reduces inflammation Leaves promotes perspiration increases milk flow Not recommended increases saliva increases urination increases heart rate  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Pilocarpine has shown to increase the rate of birth defects in animal studies. Jaborandi should not be taken during pregnancy or while breastfeeding. 2. Both jaborandi and pilocarpine may cause headaches and can irritate the stomach and cause vomiting and nausea. An overdose may cause such symptoms as flushing, profuse sweating and salivation, urinary frequency, nausea, rapid pulse, contracted pupils, diarrhea or fatal pulmonary edema. 3. The plant may induce bradycardia. Those with cardiac or circulatory conditions should not take jaborandi. 4. Jaborandi may induce dehydration due to excessive perspiration and urination. If using jaborandi, electrolyte and fluid status must be monitored and maintained. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
