    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Manaca - Parties Utilisées : Root, bark, leaf - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Manaca Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/manaca.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Acti...",
    "fullText": "84. Manaca ( Brunfelsia uniflorus ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Manaca - Parties Utilisées : Root, bark, leaf - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Manaca Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/manaca.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - terpenes  - alkaloids  - lactones  - and lipids  - It is the root  - though  - The root of manacá contains coumarins  - alkaloids - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - Manacá has a traditional use as an abortive  - therefore  - it is contraindicated for pregnant women  - vertigo  - general anesthesia  - partial paralysis of the face  - swollen tongue  - and disturbed vision - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Manacá has a traditional use as an abortive. No clinical studies have been performed to indicate its safety during pregnancy; therefore, it is contraindicated for pregnant women. 2. Manacá root is reported to have toxicity in large doses - causing excessive salivation, vertigo, general anesthesia, partial paralysis of the face, swollen tongue, and disturbed vision. Avoid dosages higher than the traditional remedy indicates. 3. Those allergic to aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) should avoid using manacá. Manacá contains salicylate and several of its derivatives. Salicylate occurs naturally in plants; for some people, too much salicylate causes problems (known as \"salicylate sensitivity\" or \"salicylate intolerance\") without being allergic to aspirin. Do not use manacá if sensitive to salicylate. 4. Manacá root contains coumarins - plant chemicals known to thin the blood. Those taking blood-thinning medications such as coumadin should use manacá only under the direction and supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner to monitor these effects. 5. The plant chemical scopoletin has been documented to inhibit monoamine oxidase. Those taking monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors should consult their healthcare practitioner before taking manacá. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Much more research is published on various chemicals found in manacá, rather than the plant itself. However, several animal studies do confirm some of its traditional uses - especially for pain and inflammation. In a 1991 clinical study with mice, manacá demonstrated pain-relieving and anti-inflammatory effects. An earlier (1977) study reported that manacá root extracts evidenced marked anti-infla... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Manacá has a long history of indigenous use for both medicine and magic. Its Brazilian common name, manacá , originated with the Tupi Indians in Brazil; they named it after the most beautiful girl in their tribe, Manacán, for its lovely flowers. It is a sacred and spiritual plant used by shamans and curanderos in the potion ayahuasca (a sacred hall... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : relieves pain prevents mutations Root reduces fever kills insects Decoction: 1/2 cup twice daily reduces inflammation promotes perspiration Capsules: 1 g twice daily detoxifies blood lowers body temperature Tincture: 1-2 ml twice daily depresses CNS moderately sedative increases urination mildly laxative moves lymphatic fluid promotes menstruation  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Manacá has a traditional use as an abortive. No clinical studies have been performed to indicate its safety during pregnancy; therefore, it is contraindicated for pregnant women. 2. Manacá root is reported to have toxicity in large doses - causing excessive salivation, vertigo, general anesthesia, partial paralysis of the face, swollen tongue, and disturbed vision. Avoid dosages higher than the traditional remedy indicates. 3. Those allergic to aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) should avoid using manacá. Manacá contains salicylate and several of its derivatives. Salicylate occurs naturally in plants; for some people, too much salicylate causes problems (known as \"salicylate sensitivity\" or \"salicylate intolerance\") without being allergic to aspirin. Do not use manacá if sensitive to salicylate. 4. Manacá root contains coumarins - plant chemicals known to thin the blood. Those taking blood-thinning medications such as coumadin should use manacá only under the direction and supervision of a qualified healthcare practitioner to monitor these effects. 5. The plant chemical scopoletin has been documented to inhibit monoamine oxidase. Those taking monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors should consult their healthcare practitioner before taking manacá. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
