    "fullText": "89. Mullaca ( Physalis angulata ) — - Famille Botanique : Solanaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Mullaca, camapu, bolsa mullaca, cape gooseberry, wild tomato, winter cherry, juá-de-capote, capulí cimarrón, battre-autour, k'u chih, 'urmoa batoto bita, cecendet, dumadu harachan, hog weed, nvovo, polopa, saca-buche, thongtheng, tino-tino, topatop, wapotok - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Mullaca Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/mullaca.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - naturally occurring chemicals including flavonoids  - alkaloids  - and many different types of plant steroids  - some of which have never before been seen in science  - is toxic to numerous types of cancer and leukemia cells  - and that it has antimicrobial properties  - The new steroids found in mullaca have received the most attention  - and many of the documented anti-cancerous - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - animal and human alike  - others use a leaf tea for asthma  - liver problems  - malaria  - hepatitis  - and rheumatism - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Mullaca exerts multifaceted anticancer effects by targeting cancer cell survival, proliferation, inflammation, and immune evasion. These properties make it a promising candidate for further development in cancer therapeutics. Various extracts of mullaca have demonstrated potent activity against a wide range of human and animal cancer cells, including those from breast, brain, colorectal, gastric, ... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : Mullaca has long held a place in natural medicine in the tropical countries where it grows. Its use by rainforest Indians in the Amazon is well documented, and its edible sweet-tart fruits are enjoyed by many rainforest inhabitants, animal and human alike. Indigenous tribes in the Amazon use a leaf infusion as a diuretic. Some Colombian tribes beli... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : kills bacteria relieves pain Infusion: 1/2 to 1 cup 1-3 kills cancer cells reduces spasms times daily kills leukemia cells lowers blood sugar Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily fights free radicals prevents ulcers Tincture: 1-3 ml twice daily relieves inflammation increases urination kills viruses reduces fever modulates immune system protects cells & organs relieves oxidative stress kills parasites reduces fibrosis prevents diabetic complications lowers blood pressure  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Several animal studies indicates this plant may lower blood pressure and one test tube study demonstrated a blood anticoagulant activity. People with blood disorders such as hemophilia, those taking heart medications or blood thinners, or those with other heart problems such as low blood pressure should not use this plant without supervision and advice of a qualified health care practitioner. Animal studies report that mullaca can lower blood glucose levels. Diabetics should use with caution and test their glucose levels regularly until they determine what effects mullaca might have on their glucose levels as diabetic medications may need adjusting. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
