    "fullText": "106. Roselle ( ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Roselle - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Roselle Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/hibiscusflower.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - anthocyanins  - flavonoids and other polyphenols - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - Do not use hibiscus flowers while breastfeeding  - If you have hemochromatosis (high iron levels)  - you should avoid using this remedy - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Do not use hibiscus flowers while breastfeeding. One rat study reported late onset of puberty in juvenile rats whose mothers consumed roselle tea prior to weaning. 2. Hibiscus flowers are a natural source of iron and have been used to treat anemia in some countries with mixed results. If you have hemochromatosis (high iron levels), you should avoid using this remedy. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : The first area of research on roselle is one of the most documented and verified – the antioxidant action of roselle calyces. Substances called free radicals and reactive oxygen species (ROS) are a product of normal life, created by metabolizing foods, and by various other chemical interactions in our bodies which are quite normal. The human body has a natural built-in antioxidant system that prod... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : All parts of the roselle shrub have been used medicinally around the world: leaves, flower calyces, seeds, and seed oil. This book will focus on the uses of the roselle’s flower calyces. The rule of thumb in most traditional medicine systems is to prepare a decoction (boiling in water for 10 minutes) if you’re using dried flower calyces and to prep... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : lowers blood pressure slows ageing Flower calyces lowers cholesterol reduces weight Decocotion: 1 cup 1-2 times daily prevents heart disease kills bacteria Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily fights free radicals kills fungi unclogs arteries kills viruses reduces inflammation protects skin protects heart prevents kidney stones increases urination protects liver lowers blood sugar protects brain enhances memory reduces diabetic complications  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Do not use hibiscus flowers while breastfeeding. One rat study reported late onset of puberty in juvenile rats whose mothers consumed roselle tea prior to weaning. 2. Hibiscus flowers are a natural source of iron and have been used to treat anemia in some countries with mixed results. If you have hemochromatosis (high iron levels), you should avoid using this remedy. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
