    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique : Amaranthaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Suma, Brazilian ginseng, pfaffia, para toda, corango-acu - Parties Utilisées :  - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Foie & Digestion (Hépato-Biliaire & Détox) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Suma Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/suma...",
    "fullText": "115. Suma ( Pfaffia paniculata ) — - Famille Botanique : Amaranthaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Suma, Brazilian ginseng, pfaffia, para toda, corango-acu - Parties Utilisées :  - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Foie & Digestion (Hépato-Biliaire & Détox) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Suma Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/suma.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Nutritionally  - suma root contains 19 different amino acids  - a large number of electrolytes  - trace minerals  - iron  - magnesium  - zinc  - vitamins A - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - as well as to treat menopause  - PMS  - and other hormonal disorders  - As such  - avoid inhalation of the root powder/dust  - Reduce dosages if these side effects are noted - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Suma has been documented to contain a significant amount of plant sterols including a significant amount of beta-ecdysterone and small amounts of stigmasterol and beta-sitosterol. These sterols might have estrogenic properties or activities and/ or cause an increase in estrogen production (not clinically proven) as this plant has been used traditionally to regulate menstrual processes, as well as to treat menopause, PMS, and other hormonal disorders. As such, it is advisable for women with estrogen-positive cancers to avoid the use of this plant. 2. The root powder has been reported to cause asthmatic allergic reactions if inhaled. When handling raw suma root powder or preparing decoctions with root powder, avoid inhalation of the root powder/dust. 3. Ingestion of large amounts of plant saponins in general (naturally occurring chemicals in suma) has shown to sometimes cause mild gastric disturbances including nausea and stomach cramping. Reduce dosages if these side effects are noted. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : In addition to the pfaffic acids having anticancerous activity, research in Japan (in 2000) reported that natural suma root had anti-cancerous activity as well. In this in vivo study, an oral administration of powdered suma root (at a dosage of 750 mg/kg) was reported to inhibit the proliferation of lymphoma and leukemia in mice and, otherwise, delay mortality. Notice, however, that this antiproli... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In South America suma is known as para toda (which means \"for all things\") and as Brazilian ginseng , since it is widely used as an adaptogen with many applications (much as \"regular\" ginseng). The indigenous peoples of the Amazon region who named it para toda have used suma root for generations for a wide variety of health purposes, including as a... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : supports hormones inhibits blood sickling Root adaptogen lowers cholesterol Decoction: 1 cup twice daily relieves pain calms nerves Capsules: 1-2 g twice daily reduces inflammation inhibits tumor growth inhibits cancer protects cells & organs kills leukemia cells oxygenates cells enhances immunity prevents muscle wasting treats erectile dysfunction prevents diseases encourages muscle growth relieves depression reduces stress  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Suma has been documented to contain a significant amount of plant sterols including a significant amount of beta-ecdysterone and small amounts of stigmasterol and beta-sitosterol. These sterols might have estrogenic properties or activities and/ or cause an increase in estrogen production (not clinically proven) as this plant has been used traditionally to regulate menstrual processes, as well as to treat menopause, PMS, and other hormonal disorders. As such, it is advisable for women with estrogen-positive cancers to avoid the use of this plant. 2. The root powder has been reported to cause asthmatic allergic reactions if inhaled. When handling raw suma root powder or preparing decoctions with root powder, avoid inhalation of the root powder/dust. 3. Ingestion of large amounts of plant saponins in general (naturally occurring chemicals in suma) has shown to sometimes cause mild gastric disturbances including nausea and stomach cramping. Reduce dosages if these side effects are noted. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
