    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Ayapana (Eupatorium ayapana) in the Tropical Plant Database of herbal remedies - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Ayapa...",
    "fullText": "122. Ayapana (Eupatorium ayapana) in the Tropical Plant Database of herbal remedies ( Eupatorium triplinerve ) — - Famille Botanique :  - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Ayapana (Eupatorium ayapana) in the Tropical Plant Database of herbal remedies - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Ayapana (Eupatorium ayapana) in the Tropical Plant Database of herbal remedies Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/ayapana.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - Ayapana is a rich source of naturally occurring coumarin chemicals  - Coumarin has clinical value as the precursor for several anticoagulant drugs  - most notably  - one widely prescribed drug called warfarin  - Ayapana also contains a natural coumarin chemical named ayapanin  - herniarin  - gerniarin  - and methylumbelliferone - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - stomach pain  - swellings  - and as a overall detoxifier  - For internal hemorrhages  - cicatrizant (closes wounds)  - astringent (dries secretions) - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : Documenté par Leslie Taylor pour ses principes phytochimiques actifs. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Researchers around the world have studied ayapana. Their body of research indicates that this rainforest natural remedy has a wide range of biological activities including antimicrobial (against bacteria, viruses and fungi), pain-relieving, anti-inflammatory, anti-ulcer, anthelmintic (expels intestinal worms), brain- liver- gastric, and heart-protective actions, anti-ulcerative colitis, photoprote... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : The Shipibo-Conibo Indians of Peru take the leaf and stem of the plant internally for colic, stomach pain, swellings, and as a overall detoxifier. They prepare a paste of the leaves to use externally on wounds and hemorrhages to stop bleeding and speed healing. For internal hemorrhages, snake bite and vomiting they juice the leaves and drink it int... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : eases nausea antiseptic leaves calms coughs promotes healing Infusion: 1 cup twice daily reduces inflammation kills cancer cells stops bleeding detoxifies protects cells and organs reduces fever relieves pain softens skin heals wounds kills bacteria aids digestion kills fungi fights free radicals relieves oxidative stress prevents ulcers  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - Ayapana leaves contain naturally occurring coumarins. Coumarin has an anti-coagulant and blood thinning effect and is a precursor to coumadin drugs. Consult with your physician before taking this plant if you are taking coumadin drugs or if coumadin anticoagulant type drugs are contraindicated for your condition. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne.  ---"
