    "excerpt": "- Famille Botanique : Urticaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Nettle - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Nettle Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/nettle...",
    "fullText": "127. Nettle ( Urtica dioica ) — - Famille Botanique : Urticaceae - Noms Usuels / Synonymes : Nettle - Parties Utilisées : Feuilles, écorce, racines ou plante entière - Catégorie Thérapeutique & Tropisme : Reins & Émonctoires (Drainage Rénal & Lithiases) - Source Primaire Rain-Tree : [Nettle Monograph on Rain-Tree.com](https://www.rain-tree.com/nettles.htm) - Principes Phytochimiques Actifs :  - histamine  - serotonin  - and choline  - In addition to these chemicals  - nettle leaf is rich in minerals  - chlorophyll  - amino acids  - lecithin - Indications & Cibles Thérapeutiques :  - Nettle has been documented to have diuretic effects - Mécanismes & Pharmacodynamie (Leslie Taylor) : 1. Actions Principales (Rain-Tree) : 1. Nettle has been documented in animal studies to lower blood pressure and heart rate. Those with heart conditions should seek the advice and supervision of a health practitioner to determine if nettle is suitable for their condition and to monitor its effects. 2. Nettle has been documented to have diuretic effects. Chronic use of this plant may be contraindicated in various medical conditions where diuretics are not advised. Chronic long-term use of any diuretic can cause electrolyte and mineral imbalances. Consult your doctor if you choose to use this plant chronically for longer than 30 days concerning possible side effects of long term diuretic use. 2. Recherche Clinique & Activité Biologique : Nettle's long-standing use as an anti-inflammatory aid for rheumatism and arthritis has been confirmed with clinical research. In several clinical studies (including a randomized double-blind placebo trial) nettle leaf extracts were documented with anti-inflammatory actions as well as to be beneficial (and better than placebo) at relieving arthritis pain and inflammation in humans. Research sugges... 3. Usage Ethnobotanique Traditionnel : In folk medicine nettle plants have been used as a diuretic, to build the blood, for arthritis and rheumatism. Externally it has been used to improve the appearance of the hair, and is said to be a remedy against oily hair and dandruff. The plant has been widely used by herbalists around the world for centuries. In the first century, Greek physicia... - Posologie & Modes d'Administration :  - Dosage Standard : reduces allergies stimulates digestion Leaves, root cleanses blood aids lactation Infusion: 1 cup 2-3 reduces inflammtion promotes menstruation times daily relieves pain kills germs Capsules: 2 g 2-3 stops hair loss lowers body temperature times daily increases urination Tincture: 2-3 ml 2-3 stops bleeding times daily dilates blood vessels lowers blood pressure heals wounds  - Infusion / Décoction : 1 cuillère à café à soupe de plante séchée par tasse d'eau chaude, infuser 10-15 min.  - Teinture : 2 à 4 ml (environ 30 à 60 gouttes) 2 fois par jour. - Contre-indications & Précautions :  - 1. Nettle has been documented in animal studies to lower blood pressure and heart rate. Those with heart conditions should seek the advice and supervision of a health practitioner to determine if nettle is suitable for their condition and to monitor its effects. 2. Nettle has been documented to have diuretic effects. Chronic use of this plant may be contraindicated in various medical conditions where diuretics are not advised. Chronic long-term use of any diuretic can cause electrolyte and mineral imbalances. Consult your doctor if you choose to use this plant chronically for longer than 30 days concerning possible side effects of long term diuretic use. - Note Vitaliste : Fiche botanique officielle Rain-Tree issue des recherches du Dr. Leslie Taylor sur la pharmacopée amazonienne."
